        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Mercury-Redstone 2 (MR-2) mission was the prelude to Alan Shepard's suborbital space flight.  MR-2 launched on January 31, 1961, carrying Ham, a four-year-old male chimpanzee.  The suborbital flight lasted a total of 16 minutes and 39 seconds, and carried the spacecraft 422 nautical miles from the launch site at Cape Canaveral, FL.  The spacecraft reached a maximum altitude of 157 statute miles.
<P>
The capsule used on MR-2 had many new innovations that had not been previously flight-tested.  These included an environmental control system, an attitude stabilization control system, a 'closed loop' abort sensing system, and a pneumatic landing bag.  Telemetry was very important to the mission, and was accomplished using eight channels of data transmitted to ground stations.  The telemetry included information on heating, pressure, noise, and vibrations.
<P>
The MR-2 launch was delayed approximately four hours because of inverter overheating.  
When the launch finally occurred, at 11:55 p.m., Ham was subjected to a 6-G accelerative force followed by a momentary exposure to 17-G's when the rockets on the escape system fired.  Ham experienced weightlessness for 7.5 minutes during the flight.  This was followed by a reverse accelerative force as the spacecraft returned toward earth.  Although this force was not as great in magnitude as the launch force, it was of longer duration.
<P>
The science objectives of the MR-2 flight included animal verification of a successful flight, collection of data on the physical and psychological demands of a space flight, evaluation of spacecraft environmental control system and bioinstrumentation, and completion of a dynamic test of operational procedures and training of support personnel.
<P>
The MR-2 capsule touched down at 12:12 p.m., about 60 miles from the nearest recovery ship.  A helicopter from the LSD Donner was dispatched, and recovered the capsule at 2:52 p.m.  The bulkhead of the capsule had been damaged by wave action and was causing the capsule to capsize.  Approximately 800 lbs of seawater had entered the capsule before it was recovered.  Ham was in good condition and even shook the hand of the commander of the LSD Donner once he got on board.
<P>
Ham's flight on MR-2 met all of its objectives, and was a significant accomplishment toward manned U.S. space flight.  The results of the flight showed no significant change in Ham's physiological state or psychomotor performance.  The mission's success increased the confidence of the astronauts and capsule engineers by proving that the demands of space flight would not be excessive for humans.  The flight also demonstrated that young chimpanzees could be trained to be highly reliable subjects for space flight studies.
<END>
